{{also|Abed|abêd}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abedde}}, {{m|enm|o bedde}}, from {{der|en|ang|on|t=in}} + {{m|ang|bedd|bedde|t=bed}}. Equivalent to {{prefix|en|a|bed|t1=in, on}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbɛd/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abed.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abed.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbɛd/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛd|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} In bed, or on the bed; confined to bed. {{defdate|First attested from 1150 to 1350.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=3}}</ref>
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Shakespeare|year=c.1564–1616|title=w:Twelfth Night|section=II, iii|passage=Not to be '''abed''' after midnight}}
#* {{RQ:nan-tws:Fielde Dictionary|en|{{l|nan-tws|倒||tr=tó̤}}|560|passage=[She] is sick '''abed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:Michael Arlen|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1519647W “Piracy”: A Romantic Chronicle of These Days]|chapter=Ep./4/2
|passage=The world was awake to the 2nd of May, but Mayfair is not the world, and even the menials of Mayfair lie long '''abed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1948|author=w:Alan Paton|title=w:Cry, the Beloved Country|location=London|publisher=Jonathan Cape|chapter=12
|passage=Who can lie peacefully '''abed''', while the darkness holds some secret?}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[childbed]]
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:William Shakespeare|year=c.1564–1616|title=w:Titus Andronicus|section=IV, ii|passage=I mean, she's brought '''a-bed'''}}

